ext4_fwrite: do not let the inode-ref release overwrite the write's error

ext4_fwrite ends with

	Finish:
		r = ext4_fs_put_inode_ref(&ref);

		if (r != EOK)
			ext4_trans_abort(file->mp);
		else
			ext4_trans_stop(file->mp);

		return r;

which discards whatever error the write itself produced. Every `goto Finish`
and `goto out_fsize` above it becomes EOK as long as releasing the inode
reference succeeds, which it normally does. The two that matter most:

  - ext4_fs_append_inode_dblk returning ENOSPC on a full volume;
  - ext4_block_writebytes returning EIO from the block device.

Both are reported to the caller as success. The only remaining evidence is
that *wcnt is short, and a caller is entitled not to inspect it after EOK.

The commit branch is the worse half. Because r has been overwritten with EOK,
the failing write takes ext4_trans_stop rather than ext4_trans_abort, so a
transaction whose write did not happen is committed to the journal instead of
being rolled back.

ext4_fread, in the same file, already keeps the two apart -- it calls
ext4_fs_put_inode_ref(&ref) and discards the result, preserving r. This makes
ext4_fwrite match, preferring the operation's error and falling back to the
release status only when the operation succeeded.

Found by filling a volume and watching ext4_fwrite return EOK having moved
zero of 4096 bytes. Covered by `make ext4-test` in sw/userspace/storage, mode
`full`, which fails against unpatched upstream with exactly that message.

diff --git a/src/ext4.c b/src/ext4.c
--- a/src/ext4.c
+++ b/src/ext4.c
@@ -1843,6 +1843,7 @@ int ext4_fwrite(ext4_file *file, const void *buf, size_t size, size_t *wcnt)
 	struct ext4_inode_ref ref;
 	const uint8_t *u8_buf = buf;
 	int r, rr = EOK;
+	int put_status;

 	ext4_assert(file && file->mp);

@@ -2006,7 +2007,17 @@ out_fsize:
 	}

 Finish:
-	r = ext4_fs_put_inode_ref(&ref);
+	/*
+	 * Astra patch 0004: do not let the inode-ref release overwrite the
+	 * error the write itself produced. ext4_fread already keeps them
+	 * apart; this path did not, so every `goto Finish` above -- an
+	 * ENOSPC out of ext4_fs_append_inode_dblk, an EIO out of
+	 * ext4_block_writebytes -- returned EOK and took the commit branch,
+	 * journalling a transaction whose write had failed.
+	 */
+	put_status = ext4_fs_put_inode_ref(&ref);
+	if (r == EOK)
+		r = put_status;

 	if (r != EOK)
 		ext4_trans_abort(file->mp);
